From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Archit Gupta <archit@accelbread.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 23:10:10 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] Recover full process names from executable paths

System Monitor tries to recover full process names by checking whether
the basename of either of the first two command-line arguments starts
with comm, the Linux process name limited to 15 characters. If neither
matches, it displays comm even when /proc/<pid>/exe has the full name.

This change additionally falls back to comparing comm with the basename
of /proc/<pid>/exe. This allows full name recovery when, for example, a
binary wrapper executes a binary with a different name while keeping the
same argv[0]. In that case, comm is the prefix of the inner binary,
whose name we can recover from /proc/<pid>/exe.
---
 src/procinfo.cpp | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/procinfo.cpp b/src/procinfo.cpp
index 15e9c19..1eeb190 100644
--- a/src/procinfo.cpp
+++ b/src/procinfo.cpp
@@ -89,6 +89,21 @@ get_process_name (ProcInfo    *info,
           g_free (basename);
         }
     }
+
+  g_autofree char *path = g_strdup_printf ("/proc/%d/exe", info->pid);
+  g_autofree char *exe = g_file_read_link (path, nullptr);
+
+  if (exe)
+    {
+      g_autofree char *basename = g_path_get_basename (exe);
+
+      if (g_str_has_prefix (basename, cmd))
+        {
+          info->name = basename;
+          return;
+        }
+    }
+
   info->name = cmd;
 }
 
